;; clojerl2 known divergences from JVM Clojure (designs/04 §5.1, D12;
;; finalized in clojerl2-025). User-facing version: docs/differences.md.
;;
;; Every deliberate difference is registered here with
;;   :reason       what clojerl2 does and why
;;   :design       the design section that decided it
;;   :alternative  what to write instead
;; and is checked two ways:
;;   * evidence: clj2_divergence_evidence_tests has one test per id
;;     (`:a/b-c' → a__b_c_test) showing the behavior; it fails if the
;;     divergence goes away, and the ids and tests must match one for one;
;;   * the corpus gate (`make certify'): a failing corpus entry may cite an
;;     id here as its cause in test/corpus-known-failures.edn.
;; clj2_divergences_tests asserts both, and that docs/differences.md
;; covers every id.
[{:id :lazy-seq/per-process-realization
  :area :lazy
  :reason "Lazy seqs and delays memoize in the process dictionary: another process walking the same lazy seq realizes it again. Values agree for pure thunks; a side-effecting thunk runs once per process. The ets memo switch (clj2_lazy:set_memo/1) realizes once across processes."
  :design "designs/03 §5.3"
  :alternative "Keep side effects out of lazy seqs (use doseq/run! for effects), or realize the seq (doall, vec) before handing it to another process."}
 {:id :lazy-seq/bounded-memo
  :area :lazy
  :reason "The memo store is bounded (clj2_lazy:memo_limit/0, default 100000 cells); past it the store is cleared, and a lazy seq walked again afterwards realizes again. Keeps long-lived processes from growing without bound."
  :design "designs/03 §6"
  :alternative "Realize a seq you walk many times into a vector; raise the limit with clj2_lazy:set_memo_limit/1 if a process legitimately holds more."}
 {:id :lazy-seq/no-chunking
  :area :lazy
  :reason "No chunked seqs: realization is one element at a time (map over a vector does not realize 32 at once). An optimisation, not semantics."
  :design "designs/03 §6"
  :alternative "Nothing to change; code that relied on chunks realizing ahead (side effects in map) should not."}
 {:id :symbol/no-metadata
  :area :reader
  :reason "Symbols are atoms and carry no metadata of their own. Metadata written directly on a def name is kept (^:private, ^:dynamic, ^{:doc ...}), and so is a defn docstring; metadata that reaches def through a macro is lost: defn- and (defn ^:private ...) define public vars."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1"
  :alternative "Write (def ^:private name (fn ...)) for a private fn; give docs as the defn docstring."}
 {:id :char/integer
  :area :strings
  :reason "A character is its integer code point; there is no separate char type: \\a is 97, (str \\a) is \"97\", (seq \"ab\") is (97 98)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1, §7"
  :alternative "Build strings from chars with (apply str (map (fn [c] (unicode/characters_to_binary (list c))) cs)) or work on strings (subs, clojure string fns) rather than chars."}
 {:id :string/code-point-indexing
  :area :strings
  :reason "Strings are UTF-8 binaries indexed by code point: count, nth and subs count code points, so (count \"😀\") is 1 (JVM: 2 UTF-16 units), and nth/subs walk the binary (O(n), JVM O(1))."
  :design "designs/03 §7"
  :alternative "Traverse with seq/reduce rather than indexing in a loop; convert once with (unicode/characters_to_list s) when you need random access."}
 {:id :number/no-ratio-arithmetic
  :area :numbers
  :reason "(/ 1 2) is 0.5: integer division that does not divide exactly gives a float; ratios read but are not computed with."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1"
  :alternative "Use quot/rem for exact integer division; keep numerator and denominator yourself where exact fractions matter."}
 {:id :number/beam-semantics
  :area :numbers
  :reason "Arithmetic follows BEAM, not the JVM (a project decision): integers are unbounded (no 64-bit long overflow, wrap-around, truncation or range checks: parse-long of a big number is that number, ^long/^double hints coerce nothing, bit ops take bignums, %x of a negative number is signed); floats compare exactly, so 0.0 and -0.0 differ; float division by zero raises badarith and ##Inf/##NaN cannot be read (BEAM floats have no Inf/NaN); comparisons and numeric predicates do not type-check their arguments (Erlang term order: (< nil 1) is false, no exception)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1; user decision 2026-10-11 (clojerl2-023)"
  :alternative "Check argument types yourself where a JVM program relied on ClassCastException; mask with (bit-and x 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) where 64-bit wrap-around was intended; test for zero before dividing floats."}
 {:id :fn/arity-at-compile-time
  :area :compiler
  :reason "A direct call to a fn var with an arity it does not have is a compile error (JVM: ArityException at run time), because vars link directly to module functions."
  :design "designs/03 §2.1a"
  :alternative "Call through a value when the arity is only known at run time: (apply f args), or a local (let [g f] (g 1 2)), raises at run time as on the JVM."}
 {:id :fn/structural-identity
  :area :compiler
  :reason "Fns are Erlang funs, which compare by value: two closures of the same fn form over equal captured values are = and identical?, and collapse as set members or map keys (JVM: every evaluation of a fn form is a distinct object)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1, §2.1"
  :alternative "Do not use fn identity as an identity token; tag values explicitly (a map with an id) instead."}
 {:id :exceptions/erlang-classes
  :area :compiler
  :reason "catch takes Erlang exception classes :error, :exit and :throw; :default, Exception and Throwable catch every class; JVM class names (ArithmeticException, IllegalArgumentException ...) are a compile error. throw raises an Erlang error. Dereferencing a future whose body threw rethrows that exception itself (JVM: wrapped in an ExecutionException)."
  :design "designs/09 §3"
  :alternative "(catch Exception e ...) or (catch :error e ...), and dispatch on the reason or (ex-data e) inside the handler."}
 {:id :protocols/type-designators
  :area :protocols
  :reason "extend-type names clj2 type tags (nil boolean keyword symbol integer float string list lazy_seq vector map set fn ... default) or deftype/defrecord names, with a few JVM aliases (Object String Long Integer Double Boolean Keyword Symbol); no class hierarchy, no reify."
  :design "designs/03 §4.1a"
  :alternative "extend-type the clj2 tag (e.g. integer instead of Long/Number); use a defrecord plus extend-type where reify was used."}
 {:id :namespaces/hot-code-two-versions
  :area :namespaces
  :reason "Redefinition follows BEAM's two-version code loading: reloading a namespace, or redefining a var at the REPL (each REPL var has a module of its own), keeps the previous version alive; fn values taken from it keep running the old code while new calls run the new one. A second redefinition purges the oldest version: fn values still holding it raise badfun when called, and a process still executing it makes the redefinition fail (old_code_in_use). JVM-style immediate global rebinding is not emulated."
  :design "designs/02 §5, designs/03 §2.2"
  :alternative "Hold the var's name rather than its value when you want the latest definition: call (f ...) directly or through a wrapper fn (fn [& args] (apply f args))."}
 {:id :namespaces/symbols
  :area :namespaces
  :reason "A namespace is its symbol: *ns*, in-ns, the-ns, find-ns and create-ns deal in symbols (there are no Namespace objects), and *ns* prints as the bare name."
  :design "designs/03 §2.1"
  :alternative "Use (ns-name *ns*) or *ns* where a name is needed; code that called Namespace methods needs the clj2 fns instead."}
 {:id :dynamic/per-process-bindings
  :area :namespaces
  :reason "Dynamic bindings live in the process dictionary. future, agent actions and bound-fn carry the caller's bindings along, as on the JVM; any other process (erlang/spawn, an Erlang callback) starts with none and sees the roots. The current namespace and *1/*2/*3/*e at the REPL are bindings too."
  :design "designs/03 §3"
  :alternative "Wrap a fn handed to erlang/spawn or to Erlang code in bound-fn, or pass the values explicitly as arguments or in messages."}
 {:id :refs/no-stm
  :area :state
  :reason "Refs and STM are not provided: ref, dosync, sync, alter, commute, ref-set and ensure raise an ex-info saying so. BEAM has no multi-object transactional memory, and a fake one would lie about isolation."
  :design "designs/03 §5.2"
  :alternative "Keep state that changes together in one value: one atom holding a map (swap! updates it atomically), or one agent / gen_server that owns it and is updated by message."} {:id :state/explicit-lifetime
  :area :state
  :reason "Mutable cells are not reclaimed when they become garbage (BEAM has no finalizers or weak references): an atom that has been written keeps its ETS row, a promise or future its result row, and an agent its process, until the VM stops. An atom that is only read costs nothing. A volatile lives in the process dictionary of the process that made it, until that process exits, and cannot be read from another process."
  :design "designs/03 §5.2"
  :alternative "Create long-lived atoms and agents once (def them) rather than per call in a long-running process; keep per-request scratch state in local bindings, loop/recur state or the process's own state, and give short-lived work its own process."}
 {:id :agents/process-per-agent
  :area :state
  :reason "Each agent is a process that runs its actions in arrival order. send and send-off share that one queue (there are no thread pools); a send made inside an action is dispatched at once rather than held until the action succeeds; shutdown-agents does nothing."
  :design "designs/03 §5.2"
  :alternative "Nothing to change for ordinary use; do not rely on sends from a failing action being dropped, nor on shutdown-agents to stop agents."} {:id :values/no-identity
  :area :state
  :reason "BEAM values have no object identity: identical? is exact equality (=:=), so two equal vectors, maps or large numbers are identical?, and compare-and-set! swaps when the current value equals the expected one (JVM: when it is the same object)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1, §5.2"
  :alternative "Do not use identical? or compare-and-set! to tell apart equal values; carry an explicit version or id in the value when you need to (e.g. {:version n ...})."}]
